// REGION 4 — A LATTICE HEAD OVER A *RECURSIVE* RELATION, UNDER RETRACTION.
// HARVESTED FROM THE INTERPRETER BEFORE P5 DELETES IT.
//
// ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND WHY IT CANNOT BE WRITTEN LATER ────────────────
// P5 deletes `stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos` (`fn epoch` :471, `fn dred` :776,
// `fn recompute`). The STATIC tier REFUSES an SCC that contains an aggregate:
// `any_agg` in `stamp_rel_incr_shape` bails, and `emit_scc_od_fns`
// (stdlib/mem/wql/rexpr_walk.logos ~:8396) returns having emitted NOTHING when
// `prm.rel_is_agg[r]`. Before this file that refusal was justified by PROSE
// ONLY — no fixture in the tree builds an SCC of {plain, aggregate}, so
// `any_agg` had been READ, never exercised, and NOTHING said what the static
// tier would have to ANSWER on the day the refusal is lifted.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE DOES NOT LIFT THE REFUSAL. It captures the SPECIFICATION the
// static tier must meet. Every cell is written so a static arm slots in beside
// the interpreter without restructuring — see THE THREE WITNESSES below.
//
// ── THE THREE WITNESSES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  (1) HAND-DERIVED CONSTANT — spelled out in the comment above each cell and
//      passed to the cell as a literal array. Derived from the graph on paper.
//  (2) INDEPENDENT ORACLE, DIFFERENT SHAPE — `sp_extremum`: depth-first
//      enumeration of SIMPLE PATHS out of the source, taking the min (or max)
//      of the path sums. It has no fixpoint, no delta, no shadow set, no
//      champion map, no absorb step, no notion of an epoch: it is a path
//      ENUMERATOR, while both engines are semi-naive relaxations. Shared code
//      between witness (2) and either engine: none.
//  (3) THE ENGINE — `IncrRec` via `q.incremental_rec(...)` + `epoch(&ZBatch)`.
//      ⚠ SLOT (3) IS THE SLOT THE STATIC TIER TAKES. When `any_agg` is lifted,
//      the emitted `<rel>_scc0_od` / `_odp` pair (or whatever an aggregate SCC
//      is lowered to) is added as witness (3b) and checked against (1) and (2)
//      by the SAME `cell_*` helpers. Witness (3) is NOT an oracle for (3b):
//      both run delete-and-rederive / recompute over the same lattice, and this
//      repo has twice watched a three-way differential pass while every side ran
//      one wrong fold.
//
// ── WHY SIMPLE PATHS ARE THE RIGHT ENUMERATION, DERIVED ────────────────────
// A derivation of `dist(v, d)` is a walk 0 → … → v whose edge weights sum to d;
// the head is a semilattice fold over ALL derivations, so the answer is the
// extremum over all WALKS. Every walk decomposes into a simple path plus a set
// of cycles.
//   • MIN, all weights > 0: a cycle only ADDS, so dropping the cycles never
//     raises the sum ⇒ min over walks == min over simple paths. (Cells L1-L4,
//     L6; L4's graph HAS a cycle, which is exactly why the argument is stated.)
//   • MAX: on a cycle the max over walks is unbounded (no least fixpoint), so a
//     max head is only meaningful on an ACYCLIC graph, where every walk IS a
//     simple path. Cell L5's graph is a DAG and the comment says so.
// Neither statement is read off an engine.
//
// ── THE SEMANTICS OF A RETRACTION AT A LATTICE HEAD, DERIVED ───────────────
// Retracting edges DE leaves PRES = ALL ∖ DE. The new answer is the extremum
// over the derivations that SURVIVE, i.e. the extremum recomputed over PRES.
// It is NOT "the old answer with the retracted contribution subtracted": min
// and max have no inverse. Three consequences this file asserts by name:
//   (a) if the current champion's ONLY support is retracted, the answer JUMPS
//       to the next surviving derivation (L1, L4, L5) — and may jump to a
//       derivation of a completely different SHAPE (L4);
//   (b) if the group loses every derivation, the group EMPTIES — there is no
//       row, not a row holding a sentinel/identity (L2). A fold that returned
//       `+inf`/0 for an empty group would be a different, wrong answer;
//   (c) if the champion VALUE keeps a surviving derivation, the answer does not
//       move even though a derivation was withdrawn (L3, L6) — the signed
//       group fold must drop MULTIPLICITY, not the value. L6 is the pure
//       multiplicity case (two derivations of the SAME value, one withdrawn);
//       L3 is the case where the surviving derivation has a different shape.
//
// ── WHAT THE INTERPRETER ACTUALLY DOES, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE SPEC ─────────
// `incr_rec.logos:571` routes EVERY aggregate retraction to `self.recompute()`
// — a full rebuild over the surviving edge set — because an additive-weight
// over-delete does not terminate on a cycle (rationale at :557-570). So `dred`
// is NEVER ENTERED for a lattice head and there is no incremental aggregate
// retraction anywhere in this repo to harvest. That is recorded here as the
// REGION-4 BLOCKER, in the named-functions shape:
//
//   BLOCKER R4-a. `fn dred` (stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos:776) has no
//     aggregate arm. The dispatch `if self.agg { self.recompute(); } else {
//     self.dred(q, &de_slots); }` (:571) makes the aggregate branch a
//     RECOMPUTE, so locals `od_rows` / `od_rhm` (:801-802) never exist for a
//     lattice head. There is NO interpreter witness for an INCREMENTAL
//     aggregate retraction; what this file pins is the ANSWER contract, which
//     is engine-independent, and a static tier that computes it incrementally
//     must meet these same cells.
//   BLOCKER R4-b. The SIGNED GROUP FOLD proper — a value→multiplicity rescan
//     under a negative weight — lives in the OTHER engine, `IncrJoin`
//     (stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos:1114 `fn epoch(&ZBatch,&ZBatch)`), reached
//     through `Query::incremental`, and is already pinned for the NON-recursive
//     case by `tests/logos/pass/wql_incr_retract_matrix.logos`. What was
//     missing, and is what L2/L3/L6 add, is the fold at a head that is inside
//     an SCC, where a withdrawn contribution can be a DERIVED row rather than
//     an EDB row.
//   BLOCKER R4-c. `IncrRec` exposes no per-group multiplicity read-back
//     (no accessor on the champion map; `snapshot()` at :1054 returns the
//     champions only). So "the multiplicity dropped from 2 to 1" is observable
//     ONLY as "the value did not move" (L6). A static tier that keeps a
//     multiplicity map should expose it, and THAT is the requirement this
//     blocker records.
//
// ── HOW MUCH WORK A BROKEN ENGINE DOES BEFORE THE CATCH ────────────────────
// Every cell asserts AFTER a full epoch has run to quiescence over the whole
// graph: the build fixpoint, the retraction, and the re-convergence. Nothing is
// caught at a first push. L4's graph carries a 3-cycle whose champion must
// DESCEND to a value derived through a completely different route, so a broken
// fold has re-relaxed the entire SCC before the assertion looks.
//
// ── CONTROL, MEASURED 2026-08-07 ───────────────────────────────────────────
// See the block above `main`.
//
// EXIT CODES: 90-93 setup; 20+2k build mismatch vs the hand constant for cell
// k; 21+2k after-retraction mismatch; 60+k the ORACLE disagrees with the hand
// constant (a bug in this file, reported separately from an engine bug). All
// ≤ 125 so none can be confused with 128+signal.
//
// exit 0 = for all six cells, hand constant == path-enumeration oracle ==
//          engine, before AND after the retraction.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.arith;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema Edge : code(0x0E5C_0000_0000_0001) {
    src: i64 = 0,
    dst: i64 = 1,
    w:   i64 = 2,
}
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ Edge };

// Node universe of every cell below. Small on purpose: the oracle enumerates
// simple paths, which is exponential in the node count and exact.
// ABSENT is the "this node has no surviving derivation" marker of the ORACLE's
// answer array — NOT of the engine's, which represents absence by having no row
// at all. Cell L2 is the assertion that those two really do agree.
fn nmax() -> i64 { return 8i64; }
fn absent() -> i64 { return 0i64 - 1i64; }

fn mk_edge(d: &Writ, s: i64, t: i64, w: i64) -> WAny {
    let e: Edge = d.make::<Edge>();
    e.src = s; e.dst = t; e.w = w;
    return WAny::ref_to((e.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn build_src(d: &Writ, hs: &Vec<WAny>, wts: &Vec<i64>) -> WAny {
    let arr: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(64i64);
    let n: i64 = hs.len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < n {
        if wts.get(i) > 0i64 { arr.push(hs.get(i)); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return WAny::ref_to(arr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8);
}

// Effective-delta discipline: clamp the source weight at 0 and emit the net.
fn src_apply(hs: &Vec<WAny>, wts: &mut Vec<i64>, batch: &mut ZBatch, idx: i64, raw: i64) {
    let old: i64 = wts.get(idx);
    let mut neww: i64 = old + raw;
    if neww < 0i64 { neww = 0i64; }
    let eff: i64 = neww - old;
    wts.set(idx, neww);
    if eff != 0i64 { batch.push(hs.get(idx), eff); }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WITNESS (2) — THE INDEPENDENT ORACLE. A DEPTH-FIRST SIMPLE-PATH ENUMERATOR.
//
// `sp_walk` walks every simple path out of `at`, carrying the running sum, and
// folds each arrival into `best[v]`. There is no fixpoint here and no delta: it
// terminates because `seen` forbids revisiting a node, so the recursion depth
// is bounded by the node count. `dir` is 3 for min and 4 for max, matching the
// registry's `rel_agg` encoding only as a mnemonic — nothing is read from the
// registry.
// ⚠ THE SOURCE NODE ITSELF IS NOT A GROUP. The rel's base body is
// `from edges e where e.src == 0`, i.e. the derivations START at 0 and the head
// is keyed by the ARRIVAL node, so `best[0]` is only set if some path returns
// to 0. Every graph below has no in-edge to 0, so it never is; the cells assert
// that too, by listing 0 as absent.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn sp_walk(esrc: &Vec<i64>, edst: &Vec<i64>, ew: &Vec<i64>, pres: &Vec<i64>,
           at: i64, acc: i64, dir: i64, seen: &mut Vec<i64>, best: &mut Vec<i64>) {
    let ne: i64 = esrc.len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < ne {
        if pres.get(i) > 0i64 && esrc.get(i) == at {
            let v: i64 = edst.get(i);
            if seen.get(v) == 0i64 {
                let s: i64 = acc + ew.get(i);
                let cur: i64 = best.get(v);
                if cur == absent() { best.set(v, s); }
                else if dir == 3i64 { if s < cur { best.set(v, s); } }
                else               { if s > cur { best.set(v, s); } }
                seen.set(v, 1i64);
                sp_walk(esrc, edst, ew, pres, v, s, dir, seen, best);
                seen.set(v, 0i64);
            }
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
}

fn sp_extremum(esrc: &Vec<i64>, edst: &Vec<i64>, ew: &Vec<i64>, pres: &Vec<i64>,
               dir: i64) -> Vec<i64> {
    let mut best: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut seen: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < nmax() { best.push(absent()); seen.push(0i64); i = i + 1i64; }
    seen.set(0i64, 1i64);          // simple paths out of the source
    sp_walk(esrc, edst, ew, pres, 0i64, 0i64, dir, &mut seen, &mut best);
    return best;
}

// ── comparison helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn vec_same(a: &Vec<i64>, b: &Vec<i64>) -> bool {
    if a.len() != b.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < a.len() { if a.get(i) != b.get(i) { return false; } i = i + 1i64; }
    return true;
}

// The engine's snapshot, read back into the SAME absent-marked array shape the
// oracle produces. A duplicate key (two rows for one group — a champion map
// that failed to absorb) is reported as a mismatch by poisoning the slot, so it
// cannot pass as agreement.
fn eng_answer(eng: &IncrRec) -> Vec<i64> {
    let mut out: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < nmax() { out.push(absent()); i = i + 1i64; }
    let r: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    let n: i64 = r.row_count();
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < n {
        let v: i64 = r.get_i64(k, 0i64);
        if v < 0i64 || v >= nmax() { return out; }        // out of universe → mismatch
        if out.get(v) != absent() { out.set(v, 0i64 - 999i64); }   // duplicate group
        else { out.set(v, r.get_i64(k, 1i64)); }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

fn lit(a: i64, b: i64, c: i64, d: i64, e: i64, f: i64, g: i64, h: i64) -> Vec<i64> {
    let mut v: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    v.push(a); v.push(b); v.push(c); v.push(d);
    v.push(e); v.push(f); v.push(g); v.push(h);
    return v;
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ONE CELL. Builds the engine on the full edge set, checks build; retracts edge
// `ridx`, checks after. Each check is hand-constant vs oracle vs engine.
// `k` is the cell index, used only to build the exit code.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn cell(qsrc: str, c: &SchemaCatalog, doc: &Writ, k: i64, dir: i64,
        esrc: &Vec<i64>, edst: &Vec<i64>, ew: &Vec<i64>,
        ridx: i64, want_before: &Vec<i64>, want_after: &Vec<i64>) -> i32 {
    let q: Query = match Query::compile(qsrc, c)
        { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 90i32; } };

    let mut hs: Vec<WAny> = vec_new::<WAny>();
    let mut wts: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < esrc.len() {
        hs.push(mk_edge(doc, esrc.get(i), edst.get(i), ew.get(i)));
        wts.push(1i64);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }

    // WITNESS (2) on the FULL set, checked against WITNESS (1) FIRST — if the
    // enumerator and the hand constant already disagree the engine is never
    // consulted, so an oracle bug can never be reported as an engine bug.
    let o_before: Vec<i64> = sp_extremum(esrc, edst, ew, &wts, dir);
    if !vec_same(&o_before, want_before) { return (60i64 + k) as i32; }

    let mut env: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    env.bind_source("edges", build_src(doc, &hs, &wts));
    let mut eng: IncrRec = match q.incremental_rec(&env)
        { Result::Ok(e) => { e } Result::Err(_e2) => { return 91i32; } };
    if eng.had_error() { return 92i32; }
    if !vec_same(&eng_answer(&eng), want_before) { return (20i64 + 2i64 * k) as i32; }

    // ── THE RETRACTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    let mut d: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    src_apply(&hs, &mut wts, &mut d, ridx, 0i64 - 1i64);
    eng.epoch(&d);
    if eng.had_error() { return 93i32; }

    let o_after: Vec<i64> = sp_extremum(esrc, edst, ew, &wts, dir);
    if !vec_same(&o_after, want_after) { return (60i64 + k) as i32; }
    if !vec_same(&eng_answer(&eng), want_after) { return (21i64 + 2i64 * k) as i32; }
    return 0i32;
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ⚠ CONTROLS, MEASURED 2026-08-07 on this tree. Each is a change to
// `stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos` at the AGGREGATE RETRACTION DISPATCH
// (`incr_rec.logos:570`, inside `fn epoch`), built with the archives dropped
// (`rm -f build/lib/logos/liblogos-*.a` + a full stdlib rebuild), then REVERTED.
// Both change the RETRACTION side only — `incremental_rec` builds through
// `fn recompute` directly, so the BUILD half of every cell still passes and a
// red is unambiguously about the retraction. Neither is a flipped condition
// sharing a body with the ground: the aggregate branch executes different code.
//
// The cells are checked one per run through a DIAGNOSTIC COPY of this file that
// prints every cell's code instead of returning the first (the copy differs only
// in the six `if rN != 0i32 { return rN; }` lines) — otherwise the first red
// hides the rest.
//
//  • CONTROL A — THE RETRACTION IS NOT APPLIED AT ALL: `if self.agg { self.recompute(); }`
//    → `if self.agg { }`. The relation keeps the champions computed over ALL.
//    PREDICTED: every cell whose answer MOVES reds; L3 and L6, whose answers do
//    not move, stay green.
//    MEASURED: L1=21  L2=23  L3=0  L4=27  L5=29  L6=0.
//
//  • CONTROL B — THE RETRACTION EMPTIES THE RELATION: the same branch replaced
//    by `self.rows = vec_new(); self.rhm = hashmap_new(); self.best = hashmap_new();
//    self.ptot = 0`. PREDICTED: every cell whose answer is NON-empty after the
//    retraction reds; L2, whose answer IS the empty relation, agrees with the
//    broken engine and stays green.
//    MEASURED: L1=21  L2=0  L3=25  L4=27  L5=29  L6=31.
//
// ⇒ EVERY CELL'S AFTER-ASSERTION IS RED UNDER AT LEAST ONE CONTROL, and the two
//   controls are complementary exactly where they must be: L2 (the group that
//   empties) is the one cell CONTROL B cannot catch, and L3/L6 (the answers that
//   must NOT move) are the two CONTROL A cannot catch.
//
// ⚠ AND THE HONEST LIMIT. L3 and L6 assert that an answer does NOT move when a
// derivation is withdrawn. The defect they exist for is an OVER-DELETE that
// phase 3 fails to repair — and the interpreter's aggregate retraction is a full
// `recompute()`, which cannot over-delete anything. So no local control on the
// interpreter can red L3/L6 for the RIGHT reason; CONTROL B reds them only by
// destroying the relation outright. Their real subject is witness (3b), the
// static tier, on the day `any_agg` is lifted. That is stated here rather than
// smoothed over: today they are two cells whose engine-side bite is weaker than
// the other four, and the reason is a property of the engine being deleted.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn main() -> i32 {
    let doc: Writ = writ_new(262144i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();

    // A single linear-recursive rel with a MIN semilattice head. The head is
    // INSIDE the recursion — `dist` joins itself — which is exactly the
    // {plain, aggregate} SCC shape `any_agg` refuses statically.
    let qmin: str = r#"rel dist(node: i64, d: i64) {
             from edges e where e.src == 0 select (e.dst, e.w);
             from dist x join edges e on e.src == x.node
                 group by e.dst aggregate d = min(x.d + e.w) select (e.dst, d);
           }
           from dist r select (r.node, r.d)"#;
    let qmax: str = r#"rel hi(node: i64, d: i64) {
             from edges e where e.src == 0 select (e.dst, e.w);
             from hi x join edges e on e.src == x.node
                 group by e.dst aggregate d = max(x.d + e.w) select (e.dst, d);
           }
           from hi r select (r.node, r.d)"#;

    // ── L1 — THE CHAMPION'S ONLY SUPPORT IS RETRACTED ──────────────────────
    // edges: 0→1 w1 [idx 0], 0→1 w4 [1], 1→2 w2 [2].   retract idx 0.
    // BEFORE, by hand: dist(1) = min(1, 4) = 1 ; dist(2) = 1 + 2 = 3.
    // AFTER,  by hand: the only surviving derivation of 1 is the w4 edge, so
    //   dist(1) = 4, and 2's ONLY derivation goes through 1 ⇒ dist(2) = 6.
    //   ⚠ min has no inverse: 1 cannot be "un-folded" out of the group; the
    //   answer is the extremum over what survives, which is a DIFFERENT row.
    let mut s1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s1.push(0i64); d1.push(1i64); w1.push(1i64);
    s1.push(0i64); d1.push(1i64); w1.push(4i64);
    s1.push(1i64); d1.push(2i64); w1.push(2i64);
    let r1: i32 = cell(qmin, &c, &doc, 0i64, 3i64, &s1, &d1, &w1, 0i64,
        &lit(absent(), 1i64, 3i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), 4i64, 6i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r1 != 0i32 { return r1; }

    // ── L2 — THE GROUP EMPTIES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    // edges: 0→1 w1 [0], 1→2 w2 [1].   retract idx 0.
    // BEFORE: dist(1)=1, dist(2)=3.
    // AFTER:  0 has no out-edge, so NO derivation survives for either group.
    //   The answer is the EMPTY relation — zero rows. A fold that emitted an
    //   identity element (+inf, or 0) for an emptied group would produce rows
    //   here, and `eng_answer` would read them back as values instead of
    //   `absent()`; that is what this cell refuses.
    let mut s2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s2.push(0i64); d2.push(1i64); w2.push(1i64);
    s2.push(1i64); d2.push(2i64); w2.push(2i64);
    let r2: i32 = cell(qmin, &c, &doc, 1i64, 3i64, &s2, &d2, &w2, 0i64,
        &lit(absent(), 1i64, 3i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r2 != 0i32 { return r2; }

    // ── L3 — A DERIVATION IS WITHDRAWN, THE VALUE SURVIVES (different shape) ─
    // edges: 0→1 w3 [0], 0→2 w1 [1], 2→1 w2 [2].   retract idx 0.
    // BEFORE: dist(2)=1 ; dist(1)=min(3 direct, 1+2 via 2)=3 — TWO derivations,
    //   both worth 3.
    // AFTER:  the direct derivation is gone, the 0→2→1 derivation survives and
    //   is still worth 3 ⇒ dist(1)=3, unchanged.
    //   ⚠ THE SNAPSHOT IS IDENTICAL BEFORE AND AFTER. An engine that
    //   over-deleted the group and failed to re-derive would lose (1,3); an
    //   engine that only decremented a multiplicity would keep it by accident.
    //   This is the cell that separates "recomputed the extremum" from "left it
    //   alone", and it is the one a final-answer oracle looks blind on unless
    //   the surviving derivation is RECURSIVE — which is why 0→2→1 is two hops.
    let mut s3: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d3: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w3: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s3.push(0i64); d3.push(1i64); w3.push(3i64);
    s3.push(0i64); d3.push(2i64); w3.push(1i64);
    s3.push(2i64); d3.push(1i64); w3.push(2i64);
    let r3: i32 = cell(qmin, &c, &doc, 2i64, 3i64, &s3, &d3, &w3, 0i64,
        &lit(absent(), 3i64, 1i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), 3i64, 1i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r3 != 0i32 { return r3; }

    // ── L4 — ⚠ THE CELL THAT MAKES REGION 4 NON-TRIVIAL ────────────────────
    // A lattice head over a RECURSIVE relation where the withdrawn contribution
    // was ITSELF DERIVED, on a graph with a CYCLE. This composes region 4 with
    // phase 3: the surviving extremum is only knowable after re-derivation.
    // edges: 0→1 w1 [0], 1→2 w1 [1], 2→3 w1 [2], 3→1 w1 [3], 0→3 w10 [4].
    //        cycle 1→2→3→1.    retract idx 2 (2→3), a MID-PATH edge.
    // BEFORE, by hand: dist(1)=1 (0→1) ; dist(2)=2 (0→1→2) ;
    //   dist(3)=min(0→1→2→3 = 3, 0→3 = 10) = 3.
    //   The champion for 3 is derived FROM the derived fact dist(2)=2 — the
    //   retracted edge is consumed at the THIRD hop, never at the first.
    // AFTER, by hand: with 2→3 gone the only derivation of 3 is the direct
    //   0→3 ⇒ dist(3)=10, a derivation of a COMPLETELY DIFFERENT SHAPE and a
    //   value more than three times larger. 1 and 2 keep their champions
    //   (0→1 and 0→1→2 use no deleted edge); the surviving 3→1 edge now offers
    //   10+1 = 11 for node 1, which loses to 1 ⇒ dist(1) stays 1.
    //   ⚠ THE CYCLE IS LOAD-BEARING: the fold must DESCEND to a fixpoint again
    //   after the champion of 3 rises, and an engine that only propagated
    //   IMPROVEMENTS (never a worsening) would keep dist(3)=3 forever.
    let mut s4: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d4: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w4: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s4.push(0i64); d4.push(1i64); w4.push(1i64);
    s4.push(1i64); d4.push(2i64); w4.push(1i64);
    s4.push(2i64); d4.push(3i64); w4.push(1i64);
    s4.push(3i64); d4.push(1i64); w4.push(1i64);
    s4.push(0i64); d4.push(3i64); w4.push(10i64);
    let r4: i32 = cell(qmin, &c, &doc, 3i64, 3i64, &s4, &d4, &w4, 2i64,
        &lit(absent(), 1i64, 2i64, 3i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), 1i64, 2i64, 10i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r4 != 0i32 { return r4; }

    // ── L5 — THE OTHER LATTICE DIRECTION: MAX, ON A DAG ────────────────────
    // ⚠ ACYCLIC ON PURPOSE. A max head over a cycle has no least fixpoint (each
    // loop increases the value without bound), so the retraction contract is
    // only stated where the answer exists.
    // edges: 0→1 w1 [0], 0→1 w5 [1], 1→2 w1 [2], 0→2 w2 [3].  retract idx 1.
    // BEFORE: hi(1)=max(1,5)=5 ; hi(2)=max(5+1, 2)=6.
    // AFTER:  hi(1)=1 ; hi(2)=max(1+1, 2)=2.
    //   Both groups move, and 2 moves because its INPUT moved — the champion at
    //   2 is re-folded from a re-derived row, not from an EDB row.
    let mut s5: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d5: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w5: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s5.push(0i64); d5.push(1i64); w5.push(1i64);
    s5.push(0i64); d5.push(1i64); w5.push(5i64);
    s5.push(1i64); d5.push(2i64); w5.push(1i64);
    s5.push(0i64); d5.push(2i64); w5.push(2i64);
    let r5: i32 = cell(qmax, &c, &doc, 4i64, 4i64, &s5, &d5, &w5, 1i64,
        &lit(absent(), 5i64, 6i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), 1i64, 2i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r5 != 0i32 { return r5; }

    // ── L6 — PURE MULTIPLICITY: TWO DERIVATIONS OF THE SAME VALUE ──────────
    // edges: 0→1 w3 [0], 0→1 w3 [1] (two DISTINCT edge nodes, same weight),
    //        1→2 w2 [2].   retract idx 0.
    // BEFORE: dist(1)=3, dist(2)=5.
    // AFTER:  the champion VALUE 3 loses one of its two supports and keeps the
    //   other ⇒ dist(1)=3, dist(2)=5, both unchanged.
    //   ⚠ THIS IS THE SIGNED GROUP FOLD'S MULTIPLICITY CASE. A fold that
    //   removed the value on the first negative weight — a set-semantics
    //   over-delete with no multiplicity — empties group 1 and cascades group 2
    //   to nothing. R4-c above records that the multiplicity itself is NOT
    //   readable from `IncrRec`, so "the value did not move" is the whole
    //   observable, and a static tier is required to expose more.
    let mut s6: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d6: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut w6: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s6.push(0i64); d6.push(1i64); w6.push(3i64);
    s6.push(0i64); d6.push(1i64); w6.push(3i64);
    s6.push(1i64); d6.push(2i64); w6.push(2i64);
    let r6: i32 = cell(qmin, &c, &doc, 5i64, 3i64, &s6, &d6, &w6, 0i64,
        &lit(absent(), 3i64, 5i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()),
        &lit(absent(), 3i64, 5i64, absent(), absent(), absent(), absent(), absent()));
    if r6 != 0i32 { return r6; }

    return 0i32;
}
